export const M1={title:'The medical assistant and professionalism',hook:'Medical assistants are among the most versatile members of the health care team: in one hour they may answer phones, verify insurance, take a health history, collect a urine specimen and assist with an exam. Employers value that range—and the professionalism that ties it together.',objectives:['Describe the Medical Assisting event, its test plan and Round Two skills.','Explain the administrative and clinical roles of the medical assistant and their scope of practice.','Compare medical assisting credentials and education paths.','Demonstrate the professional behaviors employers and patients expect.'],sections:[
['The Medical Assisting event',[
'Sponsored by the **American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA)**, Medical Assisting has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** with no time announcements at ILC. The test plan: **professionalism 4%**, **communication 6%**, **anatomy and physiology and medical terminology 10%**, **legal and ethical issues 10%**, **office procedures 10%**, **health insurance and coding 10%**, **infection control 10%**, **collecting and processing specimens 10%**, **diagnostic testing 10%**, **clinical procedures and equipment 10%** and **physical examinations 10%**. Questions come from Blesi, Wise and Kelley-Arney’s *Medical Assisting: Administrative and Clinical Competencies*, with *DHO: Health Science* used for skills.',
'**Round Two** presents a written scenario using one or more skills: **I** telephone screening (4 minutes), **II** receive a new patient and create an electronic chart (10), **III** obtain and record a health history (8), **IV** measure head and chest circumference of an infant or child (5), **V** prepare and assist with a routine physical exam (6), **VI** screen for visual acuity (5), **VII** test urine with a reagent strip (6) and **VIII** sterile gloving (3). Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils, barrier devices (non-latex gloves, gown, goggles or safety glasses and mask), **non-latex sterile surgical gloves** and a **manual watch with a second hand** for the urine test. A combined Round Two score of **70% or higher** is required for recognition; final rank adds test and skill scores, and ties go to the higher test score.',
match('Match the Round Two skill to its time',[['Telephone screening','4 minutes'],['New patient and electronic chart','10 minutes'],['Health history','8 minutes'],['Head and chest circumference','5 minutes'],['Assist with a routine physical exam','6 minutes'],['Sterile gloving','3 minutes']])]],
['Roles and scope of practice',[
'Medical assistants work mainly in **physicians’ offices, clinics and outpatient centers**. **Administrative** duties include scheduling, answering phones, registering patients, maintaining electronic health records, verifying insurance, coding and billing and managing correspondence. **Clinical** duties include rooming patients, measuring vital signs, obtaining health histories, preparing patients for exams and assisting the provider, collecting and processing specimens, performing CLIA-waived tests, ECGs and vision screening, sterilizing instruments and, where state law allows, administering medications and injections under provider supervision. Medical assistants work under the direction of a licensed provider and **do not diagnose, prescribe, interpret test results for patients or give medical advice**; state law and employer policy define their scope.',
sort('Administrative or clinical duty?',['Administrative','Clinical'],[['Scheduling appointments',0],['Verifying insurance eligibility',0],['Assigning procedure codes',0],['Measuring blood pressure',1],['Performing a urine reagent strip test',1],['Preparing an exam room and assisting the provider',1],['Creating an electronic chart',0],['Recording an ECG',1]])]],
['Education and credentials',[
'Most medical assistants complete a **certificate or diploma program** (about a year) or an **associate degree** (about two years) that includes a supervised **externship**. Credentials show competence and are often required by employers: the **Certified Medical Assistant (CMA (AAMA))**, earned by graduates of programs accredited by **CAAHEP** or **ABHES** who pass the AAMA exam and recertify every **60 months**; the **Registered Medical Assistant (RMA)** from **American Medical Technologists (AMT)**; and others such as the **Certified Clinical Medical Assistant (CCMA)** from the National Healthcareer Association. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects much faster than average job growth for medical assistants, driven by an aging population and growth in outpatient care. Many medical assistants later advance into office management, nursing or other health careers.',
match('Match the credential to its organization',[['CMA (AAMA)','American Association of Medical Assistants'],['RMA','American Medical Technologists'],['CCMA','National Healthcareer Association'],['Program accreditation','CAAHEP or ABHES'],['CMA recertification','Every 60 months'],['Externship','Supervised training in a real practice']])]],
['Professional behavior',[
'Professionalism (4% of the test) shows in **appearance**—clean, pressed scrubs or uniform, closed-toe shoes, minimal jewelry, natural nails, no strong fragrance, hair secured and a visible ID badge—and in **behavior**: punctuality, dependability, honesty, confidentiality, respect, a positive attitude, accepting constructive feedback and willingness to learn. **Teamwork** means supporting coworkers, communicating clearly and knowing when to ask for help. **Time management** and **critical thinking** let medical assistants prioritize—urgent patient needs first—and adapt when schedules change. **Empathy** (understanding another’s feelings) differs from **sympathy** (feeling pity). Professionals keep personal problems and phone use out of patient care and maintain boundaries with patients.',
sort('Professional or unprofessional?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Arriving 10 minutes early for a shift',0],['Discussing a patient in the break room',1],['Asking for clarification of an unclear order',0],['Wearing long artificial nails in a clinical role',1],['Accepting feedback and correcting an error',0],['Texting during a patient interaction',1]])]],
['Applying professionalism',[
kase('An error and a choice',{a:{text:'You realize you recorded a patient’s blood pressure in the wrong chart. No one has noticed. What should you do?',choices:[['Report the error to your supervisor and correct both records according to policy','b','Correct. Honesty and prompt correction protect patients.',true],['Say nothing because the error is small','b','Uncorrected errors can harm patients and violate ethics.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient asks you whether her lab results mean she has diabetes. What do you say?',choices:[['Explain that the provider will review and discuss the results with her','c','Correct. Interpreting results is outside the medical assistant’s scope.',true],['Tell her she probably has diabetes','c','Medical assistants do not diagnose or interpret results.',false]]},c:{text:'Your supervisor thanks you for reporting the error, and the provider calls the patient to discuss her results.',end:true}})]],
["The health care team and career growth",["Medical assistants work alongside **physicians (MD or DO)**, **nurse practitioners** and **physician assistants**, who diagnose and treat; **registered and licensed practical nurses**; **phlebotomists**, **laboratory** and **radiology** staff; **receptionists**, **billing and coding specialists** and the **office or practice manager**. Each role has a defined scope, and a clear **chain of command** tells staff whom to ask about clinical questions (the provider), policies (the office manager) or billing (the billing coordinator). Medical assistants receive tasks from providers and must decline tasks outside their scope or training politely, offering to find someone qualified.","Careers grow through **continuing education**, which credentials require, and through specialization—pediatrics, cardiology, dermatology, ophthalmology—or advancement to roles such as **clinical team lead**, **office manager**, **practice administrator** or further education in nursing, health information or other professions. A professional **résumé** highlights skills, credentials and externship experience; an **interview** is a chance to show professionalism, communication and knowledge of the practice. Memberships in organizations such as the AAMA offer continuing education, networking and advocacy for the profession.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the question to the right person in the chain of command","pairs":[["A patient asks what her test results mean","Provider"],["A coworker repeatedly arrives late","Office manager"],["A patient disputes a charge","Billing coordinator"],["You are unsure how to perform a new procedure","Supervising provider or clinical lead"],["A safety hazard in the lab area","Office manager or safety officer"],["A change in a medication order","Provider"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Test plan spans professionalism 4%, communication 6% and nine 10% areas; eight Round Two skills need 70% combined.','Medical assistants perform administrative and clinical tasks under a provider’s direction but do not diagnose, prescribe or give medical advice.','Credentials include CMA (AAMA; CAAHEP/ABHES graduates, recertify every 60 months), RMA (AMT) and CCMA; professionalism shows in appearance, honesty and teamwork.'],terms:[['Scope of practice','Duties a professional is legally allowed to perform.'],['Externship','Supervised clinical experience in a practice during training.'],['Empathy','Understanding and sharing another person’s feelings.']],mcqs:[q('Which organization sponsors the HOSA Medical Assisting event?','American Association of Medical Assistants','American Medical Association','American Dental Association','American Red Cross','The AAMA awards the CMA credential.'),q('The CMA (AAMA) must be recertified every:','60 months','12 months','10 years','Never','Through continuing education or exam.'),q('Which is outside a medical assistant’s scope?','Diagnosing a patient’s condition','Measuring vital signs','Scheduling appointments','Performing a waived urine test','Only providers diagnose.'),q('Which credential is awarded by American Medical Technologists?','RMA','CMA','RN','LPN','Registered Medical Assistant.'),q('Which item must competitors bring for the urine reagent strip skill?','A manual watch with a second hand','A smartwatch','A calculator','A microscope','Smartwatches are not allowed.'),q('Which behavior shows professionalism?','Reporting your own charting error','Hiding a mistake','Discussing patients publicly','Arriving late','Honesty protects patients.'),q('Medical assisting programs for CMA eligibility are accredited by:','CAAHEP or ABHES','HOSA','The FDA','The AMA only','Graduates may sit for the CMA exam.'),q('How many questions are on the Round One test?','50 in 60 minutes','100 in 120 minutes','25 in 30 minutes','75 in 90 minutes','No time announcements at ILC.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the administrative and clinical roles of a medical assistant, the limits of their scope of practice and the professional behaviors that build trust with patients and the team.',rubric:[['Administrative duties',['schedul','phone','register','insurance','coding','record']],['Clinical duties',['vital','history','specimen','exam','ecg','assist']],['Scope limits',['diagnose','prescribe','advice','interpret','supervision','state']],['Professional behaviors',['punctual','confidential','appearance','honest','team','empathy']]],model:'Administratively, medical assistants schedule appointments, answer and screen phone calls, register patients and create electronic charts, verify insurance, code and bill and manage records. Clinically, they room patients, measure vital signs, obtain health histories, prepare patients and assist with examinations, collect and process specimens, perform CLIA-waived tests, ECGs and vision screening, sterilize instruments and, where state law allows, administer medications under a provider’s supervision. They work under a licensed provider’s direction and do not diagnose, prescribe, interpret results for patients or give medical advice—questions like these are referred to the provider. Trust is built through punctuality and dependability, a clean professional appearance, honesty—including reporting one’s own errors—strict confidentiality under HIPAA, empathy and respect for every patient, teamwork with coworkers and continuing education to maintain credentials such as the CMA (AAMA).'},sources:[RULES,AAMA,AMT,BLS_MA]};

export const M2={title:'Communication and telephone screening (Skill I)',hook:'The telephone is often a patient’s first contact with the practice. In four minutes, Skill I asks you to greet the caller, gather seventeen pieces of information, judge urgency and route the message correctly—while sounding calm and caring.',objectives:['Apply verbal, nonverbal and therapeutic communication with patients.','Adapt communication for age, culture, language and special needs.','Screen telephone calls using the HOSA Medical Office Screening Chart.','Complete the HOSA Office Message Form following Skill I.'],sections:[
['Communication essentials',[
'Communication involves a **sender**, a **message**, a **channel**, a **receiver** and **feedback**. **Verbal** communication should be clear, concise and free of jargon; **nonverbal** cues—eye contact, facial expression, posture, gestures, tone and personal space—often carry more meaning than words. **Active listening** includes attending fully, paraphrasing, reflecting feelings, clarifying and summarizing. **Open-ended questions** (“Tell me about your pain”) gather detail; **closed questions** (“Is the pain sharp?”) confirm facts. Barriers include noise, pain, anxiety, hearing or vision loss, language differences, medical jargon and judgmental responses. Many medical assisting texts use **Maslow’s hierarchy of needs**—physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem and self-actualization—to explain that patients focused on pain or fear may not absorb teaching until basic needs are met.',
sort('Open-ended or closed question?',['Open-ended','Closed'],[['“How have you been feeling since your last visit?”',0],['“Do you have a fever?”',1],['“What brings you in today?”',0],['“Are you allergic to penicillin?”',1],['“Describe what happens when the headache starts.”',0],['“Did you take your medication this morning?”',1]])]],
['Adapting communication',[
'Effective communication is **patient-centered**. Speak to **children** at their level with simple words and involve parents; give **adolescents** privacy and respect; speak clearly and allow extra time for **older adults**, checking hearing aids and glasses. For patients with **limited English proficiency**, use a **qualified medical interpreter** rather than family members, especially children. Respect **cultural** differences in eye contact, touch, decision-making and beliefs about illness. With **angry or anxious** patients, stay calm, listen, acknowledge feelings, avoid arguing and offer solutions within policy. Recognize **defense mechanisms**—such as denial, projection, rationalization or displacement—as ways patients cope with stress. **Patient education** uses plain language, the **teach-back** method (“Can you show me how you will take this?”) and written materials at an appropriate reading level.',
match('Match the situation to the best approach',[['Patient with limited English','Use a qualified medical interpreter'],['Anxious child','Simple words and involve the parent'],['Older adult with hearing loss','Face the patient and speak clearly'],['Angry patient','Stay calm, listen and acknowledge feelings'],['Teaching a new medication schedule','Use the teach-back method'],['Adolescent patient','Provide privacy and respect']])]],
['Telephone techniques and screening',[
'Answer **promptly—by the third ring**—in a polite, pleasant manner; **identify the office and yourself** and ask, “How may I help you?” Speak **clearly**, at a **moderate rate**, with a smile in your voice, and express consideration for the caller’s needs. Never put a caller on hold without asking permission, and never leave an emergency caller on hold. The **HOSA Medical Office Screening Chart** directs actions by call type: an **emergency**—quickly record the name and complaint, keep the patient on the line while the office initiates the **911** call, stay on until 911 is contacted, then attach a note to the chart for the physician; a **prescription refill**—take a message with essential medication information including **pharmacy name and number**, attach it to the chart and place it in the physician’s message box; an **insurance or billing question**—confirm identity and entitlement, then transfer to the billing coordinator, giving the name, number and extension in case of disconnection; a **test results** or **follow-up** request—take a message and route it unless instructed to put the call through; a request to **speak to the physician** about a medical problem—determine urgency (directing true emergencies to 911) and, if the provider is unavailable, route a message.',
sort('Route the call',['Emergency: keep on line and 911','Message to provider with chart attached','Transfer to billing coordinator'],[['Caller reports crushing chest pain and sweating',0],['Patient needs a refill of a blood pressure medication',1],['Patient asks why a claim was denied',2],['Patient asks for recent lab results',1],['Parent reports a child is not breathing',0],['Patient asks about a balance on their statement',2]])]],
['The Office Message Form (Skill I)',[
'In Skill I, after answering and greeting correctly, the competitor listens and records on the **HOSA Office Message Form**: **who the message is for**, **who took it**, **date and time**, the patient’s **full name, date of birth and age**, the **caller’s name and relationship** to the patient (if the caller is not the patient), **reason for the call**, **allergies**, **call-back number**, **best time to call**, **patient’s chart number**, **medication refill**, **medication and SIG (directions)**, **pharmacy name** and **pharmacy number**, and **documented urgency**. They accurately document and **route to the provider with the appropriate level of urgency**, **attach the patient’s chart**, **sign off** on the message, close the call appropriately and **let the caller hang up first**, using appropriate communication throughout (38 points; 70% is 26.6). Repeating key details back to the caller—especially numbers, spellings and medication names—prevents errors.',
order('Telephone screening sequence (HOSA Skill I)',['Answer by the third ring, pleasantly','Identify the office and yourself; ask how you may help','Listen with consideration for the caller’s needs','Record every field on the Office Message Form','Repeat back names, numbers and medication details','Determine and document urgency','Attach the chart and route to the provider','Sign the message, close politely and let the caller hang up first'])]],
['Applying telephone judgment',[
kase('A refill call that changes',{a:{text:'Mrs. Lee calls for a refill of her blood pressure medication. What must you record besides her identity and the medication?',choices:[['Medication and SIG, pharmacy name and number, allergies, call-back number and best time to call','b','Correct. These are required fields for a refill message.',true],['Only her name','b','The provider needs complete information to act.',false]]},b:{text:'While talking, she mentions she has had a severe headache and blurred vision since this morning. What do you do?',choices:[['Recognize possible urgency, follow the screening chart and route urgently or initiate emergency care per policy','c','Correct. New severe symptoms change the urgency of the call.',true],['Finish the refill message and say goodbye','c','Ignoring new symptoms could delay emergency care.',false]]},c:{text:'You mark the message urgent, alert the provider immediately and document the call. The provider advises her to seek emergency evaluation.',end:true}})]],
["Difficult conversations and written communication",["Some conversations are hard: a patient grieving a loss, frightened by a new diagnosis or frustrated by a long wait. Listen without interrupting, acknowledge feelings (“I can see this is upsetting”), avoid false reassurance and offer what you can within your role—a quiet room, information about the next step or a call from the provider. Diagnoses and bad news are delivered by the provider, but the medical assistant’s compassion shapes the patient’s experience. Recognize the **stages of grief**—denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance—which patients and families may move through in any order.","**Written communication** is part of professionalism. Business letters are often written in **full block style** (all lines flush left) with a date, inside address, salutation, body, complimentary closing and signature block, proofread for spelling and grammar. **Email** and **patient portal** messages use a clear subject line, professional tone, no slang and the minimum necessary PHI; practices send PHI only through secure, encrypted systems approved by policy. Messages are documented in the record when they relate to care.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Appropriate written communication?","buckets":["Appropriate","Inappropriate"],"items":[["A secure portal message confirming an appointment",0],["A proofread letter in full block style",0],["Texting a patient’s diagnosis from a personal phone",1],["Using slang and emojis in a referral letter",1],["A clear subject line in an email to a referring office",0],["Including full lab results in an unsecured email",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Use active listening, open and closed questions, plain language and teach-back; adapt for age, culture, language and emotion.','Answer by the third ring, identify the office and yourself, speak clearly at a moderate rate; follow the HOSA screening chart (emergency → keep on line and 911).','Skill I: record all Office Message Form fields (including SIG and pharmacy number), document urgency, attach the chart, sign and let the caller hang up first.'],terms:[['SIG','Directions for taking a medication.'],['Teach-back','Asking patients to explain or demonstrate instructions to confirm understanding.'],['Active listening','Attending fully and reflecting the speaker’s message.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill I, the phone should be answered:','By the third ring','After the fifth ring','Only after finishing current work','By voicemail','Promptness is scored.'),q('A caller reports an emergency. According to the HOSA screening chart, you should:','Keep the patient on the line while the office initiates a 911 call','Put them on hold','Ask them to call back','Schedule an appointment','Stay on the line until 911 is contacted.'),q('A prescription refill message must include:','The pharmacy name and number','The patient’s salary','The insurance group number only','The provider’s home phone','The pharmacy is needed to send the prescription.'),q('Who should hang up first at the end of a call?','The caller','The medical assistant','Whoever is busier','It does not matter','Let the caller end the call.'),q('Which is an open-ended question?','“Tell me about your symptoms.”','“Do you smoke?”','“Is it your left arm?”','“Are you allergic to latex?”','It invites detail.'),q('For a patient with limited English proficiency, use:','A qualified medical interpreter','The patient’s young child','Louder speech','Medical jargon','Interpreters ensure accuracy.'),q('An insurance billing question should be:','Transferred to the billing coordinator after confirming identity','Answered with a diagnosis','Ignored','Sent to the physician','Give the extension in case of disconnection.'),q('The teach-back method asks the patient to:','Explain or demonstrate instructions in their own words','Read a pamphlet silently','Sign a form only','Repeat the provider’s name','It confirms understanding.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient calls asking for a refill of a medication and mentions new chest discomfort. Describe how you would handle the call following HOSA Skill I and the Medical Office Screening Chart.',rubric:[['Greeting and voice',['third ring','identify','how may i help','clear','moderate']],['Message form fields',['name','date of birth','allergies','pharmacy','sig','call-back','chart']],['Urgency judgment',['urgent','chest','911','emergency','screening chart']],['Routing and closing',['attach','route','provider','sign','hang up first']]],model:'I would answer by the third ring, identify the office and myself and ask how I may help, speaking clearly at a moderate rate and showing concern. I would record the message for the provider, my name, the date and time, the patient’s full name, date of birth and age, the caller’s name and relationship if not the patient, the reason for the call, allergies, call-back number and best time to call, chart number, the medication and SIG, and the pharmacy name and number, repeating key details back. Because the patient mentions new chest discomfort, I would assess urgency using the screening chart: if the symptoms suggest an emergency, I would keep the patient on the line while the office initiates a 911 call and stay on until 911 is contacted; otherwise I would mark the message urgent and alert the provider immediately. I would document the urgency, attach the patient’s chart, route it to the provider, sign off on the message, close the call politely and let the caller hang up first.'},sources:[RULES,AAMA,HIPAA]};

export const M3={title:'Legal and ethical issues in the medical office',hook:'If a medical assistant makes a derogatory statement about another health professional’s practice, the assistant may be liable for defamation. Law and ethics (10% of the test) shape every interaction—from consent and confidentiality to what you say in the waiting room.',objectives:['Distinguish intentional and unintentional torts and the elements of negligence.','Explain types of consent and patients’ rights.','Apply HIPAA privacy rules and the right of access to records.','Describe mandatory reporting, scope of practice and ethical principles.'],sections:[
['Law in health care',[
'**Laws** are rules enforced by government; **ethics** are standards of right conduct. **Criminal law** addresses offenses against society (such as fraud or practicing without a license); **civil law** addresses disputes between individuals, including **torts**—wrongful acts that cause harm. **Intentional torts** include **assault** (threatening harm), **battery** (touching without consent), **false imprisonment** (restraining someone against their will), **defamation** (false statements that damage reputation—**slander** if spoken, **libel** if written), **invasion of privacy** and **fraud**. **Unintentional torts** include **negligence** and **malpractice** (professional negligence). Under **respondeat superior** (“let the master answer”), employers can be liable for employees’ actions within their job duties, but employees remain responsible for their own conduct.',
match('Match the tort to its example',[['Defamation','Telling patients another physician is incompetent'],['Battery','Giving an injection after the patient refused'],['Assault','Threatening to restrain a patient'],['False imprisonment','Not letting a patient leave the office'],['Invasion of privacy','Posting a patient’s photo online'],['Negligence','Failing to report a critical lab value, causing harm']])]],
['Negligence and malpractice',[
'Negligence is proven by the **four Ds**: **duty** (a provider–patient relationship existed), **dereliction** (the standard of care was breached), **direct cause** (the breach caused the injury) and **damages** (the patient was harmed). Malpractice risk is reduced by staying within your **scope of practice**, following office policies and protocols, documenting accurately and promptly, communicating clearly, following up on test results, obtaining proper consent, keeping skills current and treating patients courteously—patients are less likely to sue providers they trust. The **statute of limitations** sets the time within which a lawsuit can be filed. **Good Samaritan laws** protect people who give emergency care in good faith outside the workplace, within their training.',
order('Arrange the four Ds of negligence',['Duty: a care relationship existed','Dereliction: the standard of care was breached','Direct cause: the breach caused the injury','Damages: the patient suffered harm'])]],
['Consent and patients’ rights',[
'**Implied consent** is shown by actions—a patient rolling up a sleeve for a blood pressure check—or presumed in emergencies. **Expressed consent** is stated in words or writing. **Informed consent** is required for procedures with risk: the provider explains the procedure, risks, benefits, alternatives and the risks of refusing, and the patient (or legal guardian) agrees voluntarily; the medical assistant may witness the signature. **Minors** generally need a parent’s or guardian’s consent, except **emancipated minors** and specific services allowed by state law. Patients have the right to **refuse** treatment, which is documented, often with a signed refusal form. **Advance directives**—living wills and durable powers of attorney for health care—state wishes for future care.',
sort('Type of consent',['Implied','Expressed or informed'],[['Patient holds out an arm for a blood draw',0],['Unconscious patient receiving emergency care',0],['Patient signs a form before minor surgery after hearing the risks',1],['Patient says, “Yes, you may give me the flu shot.”',1],['Patient sits on the exam table when asked',0]])]],
['Confidentiality and HIPAA',[
'The **Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)** Privacy Rule protects **protected health information (PHI)**—any individually identifiable health information. PHI may be used and shared for **treatment, payment and health care operations** without written authorization; most other releases require the patient’s **signed authorization**. Practices give patients a **Notice of Privacy Practices**, and staff access only the **minimum necessary** information for their job. The HIPAA **Security Rule** requires safeguards for electronic PHI: passwords, automatic log-offs, screens positioned away from public view and encryption. Patients have the **right to access** and receive copies of their records, request corrections and receive an accounting of certain disclosures. Breaches must be reported according to the Breach Notification Rule. In practice: speak quietly, never discuss patients in public areas, use sign-in sheets that do not reveal diagnoses and verify identity before releasing information.',
chk('A patient’s employer calls asking whether the patient was seen today. What should the medical assistant do?',['Decline to share any information without the patient’s written authorization','Confirm the visit and diagnosis','Share only the diagnosis','Transfer the call to the physician to answer'],'Employers are not entitled to PHI without authorization.')]],
['Reporting, regulation and ethics',[
'Health care workers must **report** certain information by law, such as suspected **child, elder or domestic abuse**, certain **communicable diseases**, births and deaths, and some injuries such as gunshot wounds, according to state requirements. **Controlled substances** are regulated by the **Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA)** in five **schedules** (I has the highest abuse potential and no accepted medical use; V the lowest), with strict rules for storage, records and prescriptions. **OSHA** protects worker safety, and **CLIA** regulates laboratory testing. Ethics guide decisions when laws do not: principles include **autonomy**, **beneficence**, **nonmaleficence**, **justice** and **fidelity**, and the **AAMA Code of Ethics** commits medical assistants to human dignity, confidentiality, honesty and continuing education.',
kase('A difficult conversation',{a:{text:'While rooming a child, you notice several bruises in different stages of healing on the back, and the parent’s explanation does not fit. What should you do?',choices:[['Document objectively and report your concern to the provider, following mandatory reporting law','b','Correct. Suspected abuse must be reported.',true],['Say nothing because it is not your business','b','Health care workers are mandated reporters.',false]]},b:{text:'A coworker says the previous doctor “was a quack” in front of patients. Why is this a problem?',choices:[['It could be defamation and is unprofessional','c','Correct. Derogatory statements about other professionals can create liability.',true],['It is acceptable free speech at work','c','Statements that harm reputation can lead to defamation claims.',false]]},c:{text:'The provider reports the suspected abuse to child protective services, and the office manager reminds staff about professional conduct.',end:true}})]],
["Releasing records and workplace law",["Requests for records follow policy. Under HIPAA, patients have the **right to access** and obtain copies of their own records, generally within **30 days** of the request (with one possible 30-day extension), and may be charged a reasonable, cost-based fee. Releases to others—employers, attorneys, family members—require the patient’s **signed authorization** unless a specific legal exception applies, such as a valid court order or required public health reporting. Verify identity before releasing information, send only what is authorized and document every release. Records are **retained** according to state and federal requirements, and destroyed securely (shredding or certified electronic deletion) when retention ends.","Workplace laws protect employees and patients. **OSHA** sets safety standards; the **Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)** requires reasonable accommodations and accessible facilities; **Title VII of the Civil Rights Act** prohibits discrimination and harassment based on race, color, religion, sex or national origin; and **CLIA** governs laboratory testing. The **Patient Self-Determination Act** requires many facilities to inform patients about advance directives. Medical assistants report harassment, unsafe conditions or suspected fraud through proper channels without fear of retaliation.",{"t":"check","q":"A patient requests a copy of her own medical records. What applies under HIPAA?","options":["She has a right to access them, generally within 30 days","She must get the provider’s permission first","Records can only be released to attorneys","Patients cannot see their own records"],"why":"Patients have a legal right of access."}]]
],takeaways:['Intentional torts include assault, battery, false imprisonment, defamation (slander/libel) and invasion of privacy; negligence requires duty, dereliction, direct cause and damages.','Consent may be implied, expressed or informed; patients may refuse; minors usually need guardian consent.','HIPAA allows PHI use for treatment, payment and operations; other releases need authorization; report abuse and required diseases; DEA schedules I–V.'],terms:[['Tort','A civil wrong that causes harm.'],['Protected health information','Individually identifiable health information covered by HIPAA.'],['Respondeat superior','Employer liability for employees’ acts within their duties.']],mcqs:[q('A medical assistant makes a derogatory statement about another professional’s practices. This may be:','Defamation','Assault','Battery','Invasion of privacy','A Blesi sample question.'),q('Touching a patient without consent is:','Battery','Assault','Slander','Libel','Assault is the threat.'),q('Which is NOT one of the four Ds of negligence?','Diagnosis','Duty','Damages','Direct cause','The fourth is dereliction.'),q('A patient rolls up a sleeve for a blood pressure reading. This is:','Implied consent','Informed consent','Written consent','No consent','Actions show agreement.'),q('Under HIPAA, PHI may be shared without authorization for:','Treatment, payment and health care operations','Marketing','An employer’s request','A neighbor’s request','Other uses need authorization.'),q('Written defamation is called:','Libel','Slander','Fraud','Assault','Spoken defamation is slander.'),q('Which agency regulates controlled substances?','DEA','OSHA','CLIA','CDC','Schedules I–V.'),q('Suspected child abuse should be:','Reported according to mandatory reporting laws','Ignored without proof','Discussed with other parents','Posted online','Health workers are mandated reporters.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a medical assistant protects patients and the practice legally, addressing negligence, consent, HIPAA and mandatory reporting.',rubric:[['Negligence and prevention',['duty','dereliction','direct cause','damages','scope','document']],['Consent',['implied','expressed','informed','refuse','minor','witness']],['HIPAA',['phi','treatment','payment','operations','authorization','minimum necessary']],['Reporting and ethics',['abuse','communicable','report','ethic','defamation']]],model:'Negligence requires duty, dereliction of the standard of care, direct cause and damages, so I would reduce risk by staying within my scope, following protocols, documenting accurately and promptly, following up on results and communicating clearly and courteously. I would make sure appropriate consent is obtained: implied consent for routine care shown by the patient’s actions, expressed consent for simple procedures and informed consent—explained by the provider and witnessed if needed—for procedures with risk, respecting the right to refuse and obtaining a guardian’s consent for minors. Under HIPAA I would share PHI only for treatment, payment and operations or with signed authorization, access only the minimum necessary, protect electronic records with passwords and screen privacy and verify identity before releasing information. I would report suspected abuse and required communicable diseases as state law requires, avoid derogatory comments about others that could be defamation and follow ethical principles of autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence and justice.'},sources:[RULES,HIPAA,AAMA]};

export const M4={title:'Office procedures, records and the EHR (Skill II)',hook:'A new patient arrives with an insurance card and a stack of questions. Within ten minutes the medical assistant must greet them, scan the card, enter dozens of fields into the electronic health record and verify coverage—Skill II. Office procedures make up 10% of the test.',objectives:['Compare appointment scheduling methods and manage the daily schedule.','Describe the medical record, SOAP notes and subjective and objective information.','Use the electronic health record securely and accurately.','Receive a new patient and create an electronic chart following Skill II.'],sections:[
['Scheduling appointments',[
'Practices choose scheduling methods to fit their flow. **Time-specified (stream) scheduling** gives each patient a set time based on visit type. **Wave scheduling** books several patients at the start of each hour and sees them in order of arrival; **modified wave** books patients at the top of the hour and others at intervals. **Double-booking** schedules two patients at the same time and should be used sparingly. **Clustering (categorization)** groups similar visits, such as physicals or immunizations, at set times. **Open hours** see patients as they arrive. A **matrix** blocks times when providers are unavailable. New-patient visits usually take longer than established-patient visits. Medical assistants document **no-shows** and cancellations in the record, triage requests by urgency and leave buffer time for emergencies.',
match('Match the scheduling method to its description',[['Stream (time-specified)','Each patient has an individual set time'],['Wave','Several patients booked at the start of each hour'],['Modified wave','Patients at the top of the hour and at intervals after'],['Clustering','Similar visit types grouped together'],['Double-booking','Two patients booked in one time slot'],['Matrix','Blocking times the provider is unavailable']])]],
['The medical record',[
'The **medical record** is a legal document that supports continuity of care, billing and quality. It contains **administrative information** (demographics, insurance, consent and authorization forms) and **clinical information** (history, exam findings, test results, diagnoses, treatment plans, medication lists, progress notes and correspondence). Information the patient provides—such as the **medical history and chief complaint**—is **subjective**; information observed or measured—vital signs, exam findings and test results—is **objective**. Many providers document in **SOAP** format: **S**ubjective, **O**bjective, **A**ssessment (diagnosis) and **P**lan. The **problem-oriented medical record (POMR)** organizes care around a problem list. Paper records are filed **alphabetically** or **numerically** (terminal-digit filing groups numbers by their last digits). Entries are timely, accurate, objective and signed; errors are corrected per policy, never erased or deleted. Records are retained according to state and federal rules.',
sort('Subjective or objective?',['Subjective','Objective'],[['Chief complaint in the patient’s words',0],['Blood pressure 132/84',1],['Patient’s reported medical history',0],['Urine reagent strip results',1],['“I feel dizzy when I stand up.”',0],['Weight 165 lb',1]]),
chk('Information in the record that the patient provides, including the medical history and chief complaint, is classified as:',['Subjective','Objective','Administrative','Identifiable'],'A Blesi sample question.')]],
['The electronic health record',[
'An **electronic health record (EHR)** stores patient information digitally and can be shared securely across providers. EHRs support **e-prescribing**, lab interfaces, reminders for preventive care, **clinical decision support**, patient **portals** for messaging, results and scheduling, and data for quality reporting. Security is essential: each user has a unique **login and password**, never shared; users log off or lock screens when stepping away; access is limited by role; audit trails track who views records; and data are backed up. Accuracy matters because errors copy forward: verify identity with **two identifiers**, avoid copy-and-paste of old notes and review entries before saving. The EHR also manages **practice management** tasks such as registration, scheduling, eligibility checks and billing.',
match('Match the EHR feature to its purpose',[['Unique login and password','Identifies the user and protects access'],['Audit trail','Records who accessed or changed the record'],['Patient portal','Lets patients message, view results and schedule'],['E-prescribing','Sends prescriptions electronically to pharmacies'],['Eligibility check','Verifies insurance coverage before the visit'],['Clinical decision support','Alerts and reminders that guide care']])]],
['Receiving a new patient (Skill II)',[
'In Skill II (10 minutes), the competitor **verbalizes signing on to the computer** with appropriate login and password; **greets the patient promptly and courteously**, calling them by **full name** with eye contact; **asks for the insurance card**; provides a clipboard, pen and a blank **HOSA Medical Office Registration Form** and instructs the patient to complete it; **scans the insurance card** (simulated), electronically **attaches it to the EHR** (verbalized) and **returns the card**. After receiving the completed handwritten form, the competitor opens a blank electronic registration form and accurately enters every field: **full and preferred name, street address, city/state/zip, phone, OK to leave detailed message, email, date of birth, last four digits of the Social Security number, marital status, preferred language, race, ethnicity, religion, organ donor, new to practice, referred by, primary physician**, **emergency contact** (name, relationship, phone), **preferred method of communication**, **insured’s information** (subscriber name, birthdate, relation to patient, phone, health plan name and address, group number, subscriber number, eligibility date, co-pay) and **employer information** (name, address, phone, occupation). They verbalize that the form is **signed and dated** and add the original to the chart, **verify insurance coverage with an eligibility check** and communicate appropriately throughout (48 points; 70% is 33.6).',
order('Receive a new patient (HOSA Skill II)',['Verbalize logging in with your own credentials','Greet the patient by full name with eye contact','Ask for the insurance card','Give the registration form, clipboard and pen with instructions','Scan the card, attach it to the EHR and return it','Enter every registration field accurately','Confirm the form is signed and dated; add it to the chart','Run an insurance eligibility check'])]],
['Front-office systems and courtesy',[
'The front office sets the tone of the visit. Greet patients promptly even when on the phone (a smile or gesture), protect privacy at the check-in window, collect co-pays and update demographics and insurance at every visit, keep the waiting area clean and safe and inform patients of delays. Equipment includes computers, multifunction printers and scanners, fax, telephone systems and point-of-sale devices. Medical assistants also manage **correspondence** (professional letters and email in business format), **supplies and inventory**, petty cash and opening and closing procedures. Accuracy in data entry prevents denied claims and misidentified patients.',
kase('A registration problem',{a:{text:'A new patient’s handwritten form lists a different date of birth than her insurance card. What should you do?',choices:[['Politely verify the correct date of birth with the patient and check the card before entering it','b','Correct. Accurate identifiers prevent claim denials and patient mix-ups.',true],['Enter whichever date you prefer','b','Guessing leads to errors.',false]]},b:{text:'The eligibility check shows her coverage ended last month. What next?',choices:[['Inform her privately, ask for any new coverage and explain self-pay options per policy','c','Correct. Handle financial issues tactfully and privately.',true],['Announce it in the waiting room','c','Financial information is private.',false]]},c:{text:'She provides a new card from her employer’s plan; you update the EHR and rerun eligibility successfully.',end:true}})]],
["Managing patient flow",["A smooth schedule depends on **triage** and planning. When patients call for appointments, the medical assistant asks the reason for the visit to choose the correct appointment type and length and to identify urgent problems that need same-day care or immediate referral to emergency services, following office protocols. **Buffer** time absorbs emergencies and walk-ins. When patients arrive **late**, policies may require rescheduling or seeing them as time allows; **no-shows** are documented in the record and may receive a reminder of the policy. **Reminder** calls, texts and portal messages reduce no-shows. When the provider is delayed, patients are informed of the wait and offered the choice to reschedule.","**Referrals** to specialists require sending the necessary records, verifying insurance requirements such as preauthorization and confirming that the appointment occurred and results were received. Tracking systems ensure that ordered tests and referrals are completed and results reviewed—one of the most important ways the office prevents missed diagnoses.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Handle an appointment request","steps":["Greet the caller and identify the patient","Ask the reason for the visit","Decide urgency using office protocols","Choose the appointment type and length","Offer times and confirm the choice","Give preparation instructions and confirm contact information"]}]]
],takeaways:['Scheduling methods: stream, wave, modified wave, double-booking, clustering and open hours; document no-shows.','The record is a legal document; patient-provided history and chief complaint are subjective, measurements objective; SOAP = subjective, objective, assessment, plan.','Skill II: log in, greet by full name, scan and return the insurance card, enter every registration field, confirm signature and run an eligibility check.'],terms:[['EHR','Electronic health record shared securely across care settings.'],['SOAP note','Subjective, Objective, Assessment and Plan documentation format.'],['Eligibility check','Verification that insurance coverage is active.']],mcqs:[q('Which scheduling method books several patients at the start of each hour?','Wave scheduling','Stream scheduling','Clustering','Open hours','Patients are seen in arrival order.'),q('The chief complaint and medical history the patient provides are:','Subjective','Objective','Administrative','Identifiable','Measurements are objective.'),q('In SOAP notes, the “A” stands for:','Assessment','Allergies','Administration','Appointment','Assessment is the diagnosis or impression.'),q('In Skill II, after scanning the insurance card, the competitor:','Attaches it to the EHR and returns it to the patient','Keeps the card','Shreds the card','Mails it to the insurer','Return the original.'),q('Why must EHR users never share passwords?','Logins identify who accessed records and protect PHI','Sharing is faster','It is required by insurers','Passwords are optional','Audit trails depend on unique logins.'),q('Which step completes Skill II?','Running an insurance eligibility check','Taking the blood pressure','Scheduling surgery','Coding the visit','Coverage is verified.'),q('Terminal-digit filing groups records by:','The last digits of the record number','Patient’s first name','Date of birth','Insurance company','It is a numeric system.'),q('How many digits of the Social Security number are entered in Skill II?','The last four','All nine','None','The first three','Limiting identifiers protects privacy.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would receive a new patient and create an electronic chart following HOSA Skill II, and explain how you would protect accuracy and privacy.',rubric:[['Greeting and card handling',['login','greet','full name','insurance card','scan','return']],['Registration fields',['address','date of birth','emergency contact','subscriber','group number','employer']],['Completion steps',['signed','dated','chart','eligibility']],['Accuracy and privacy',['two identifiers','verify','password','privacy','hipaa','log off']]],model:'I would log in with my own username and password, greet the patient promptly by full name with eye contact and ask for the insurance card. I would give a clipboard, pen and blank registration form with instructions, scan the card, attach it to the EHR and return it. From the completed form, I would enter every field: full and preferred names, address, phone and permission to leave messages, email, date of birth, last four digits of the Social Security number, marital status, preferred language, race, ethnicity, religion, organ donor status, whether new to the practice, referral source and primary physician, emergency contact, preferred communication method, all subscriber and plan information including group and subscriber numbers, eligibility date and co-pay, and employer details. I would confirm the form is signed and dated, add the original to the chart and run an eligibility check. To protect accuracy, I would verify identifiers such as name and date of birth against the card and ask the patient to clarify discrepancies; to protect privacy, I would keep my screen out of public view, speak quietly, never share my login and lock or log off when I step away.'},sources:[RULES,ONC_EHR,HHS_ACCESS]};

export const M5={title:'Health insurance, billing and coding',hook:'If a policy has a $75 deductible, the patient pays that amount each year before the insurance company pays. Insurance terms, claims and codes decide whether a practice is paid—and whether patients understand their bills. Insurance and coding make up 10% of the test.',objectives:['Define deductible, copayment, coinsurance, premium and other insurance terms.','Compare government and private insurance and managed care plans.','Describe the billing cycle, claim forms and reasons for denials.','Explain ICD-10-CM, CPT and HCPCS coding and compliance.'],sections:[
['Insurance terms',[
'A **premium** is the amount paid (often monthly) to keep coverage. A **deductible** is the amount the patient must pay **each year** for covered services **before the insurance company begins to pay**. A **copayment (co-pay)** is a fixed amount paid at each visit, such as $25. **Coinsurance** is a percentage split after the deductible—for example, the plan pays 80% and the patient 20%. The **out-of-pocket maximum** caps what the patient pays in a year. The **subscriber** (policyholder) holds the policy; **dependents** are covered under it. **Coordination of benefits** determines which plan pays first when a patient has two policies. **Preauthorization** (prior authorization) is the plan’s approval required before certain services. An **explanation of benefits (EOB)** tells the patient what was paid and what they owe; the practice receives a **remittance advice**.',
match('Match the term to its meaning',[['Deductible','Amount paid each year before insurance pays'],['Copayment','Fixed amount paid at each visit'],['Coinsurance','Percentage the patient pays after the deductible'],['Premium','Regular payment to keep coverage'],['Preauthorization','Plan approval required before a service'],['Explanation of benefits','Statement of what the plan paid and what is owed']]),
{t:'example',title:'Calculate the patient’s share',prompt:'A patient has a $500 annual deductible, already met, and 80/20 coinsurance. The allowed charge for a procedure is $1,200. How much does the patient owe, and how much does the plan pay?',steps:['The deductible has been met, so coinsurance applies to the full allowed amount.','Patient share: 20% × $1,200 = $240.','Plan share: 80% × $1,200 = $960.'],answer:'The patient owes $240 and the plan pays $960. (Amounts are invented for practice.)'}]],
['Types of coverage',[
'**Medicare** is the federal program for people **65 and older**, some younger people with disabilities and people with end-stage renal disease: **Part A** covers hospital care, **Part B** physician and outpatient services, **Part C (Medicare Advantage)** private plans that combine coverage and **Part D** prescription drugs. **Medicaid** is a joint federal–state program for people with low income; **CHIP** covers children in families who earn too much for Medicaid but cannot afford private insurance. **TRICARE** covers active-duty and retired military members and their families, and **CHAMPVA** certain veterans’ families. **Workers’ compensation** covers work-related injuries. **Managed care** plans control costs: a **health maintenance organization (HMO)** usually requires a **primary care provider (gatekeeper)**, **referrals** to specialists and use of in-network providers; a **preferred provider organization (PPO)** allows out-of-network care at higher cost without referrals; a **point-of-service (POS)** plan blends both. **High-deductible plans** are often paired with health savings accounts.',
sort('Which program?',['Medicare','Medicaid or CHIP','TRICARE or workers’ compensation'],[['A 70-year-old retiree’s physician visits (Part B)',0],['Prescription coverage through Part D',0],['A low-income family’s coverage from a federal–state program',1],['Coverage for a child whose family earns slightly too much for Medicaid',1],['A soldier’s spouse’s visit',2],['An injury that happened on the job',2]])]],
['The billing cycle and claims',[
'The **revenue cycle** runs from scheduling to payment: preregistration and **eligibility verification**, check-in and collection of co-pays, documentation of the encounter, **coding** of diagnoses and procedures, **charge entry**, submission of a **claim**, payment posting, follow-up on denials and **patient billing** and collections. Professional services are billed on the **CMS-1500** claim form (or its electronic equivalent, the 837P); hospitals use the **UB-04**. Electronic claims are often sent through a **clearinghouse** that checks for errors. Common reasons for **denials** include incorrect patient information, inactive coverage, missing preauthorization, coding errors, services not covered and missed filing deadlines. A **clean claim** has no errors and is paid promptly. Practices may write off amounts above the plan’s **allowed amount** when they are participating providers.',
order('Order the revenue cycle',['Verify eligibility before the visit','Check in the patient and collect the co-pay','Document the encounter','Code diagnoses and procedures','Submit a clean claim','Post the payment and review the remittance','Bill the patient for any balance'])]],
['Coding: ICD-10-CM, CPT and HCPCS',[
'**Diagnosis codes** answer “why” the patient was seen. **ICD-10-CM** codes are **alphanumeric**, **3 to 7 characters** long, beginning with a letter (for example, E11.9 for type 2 diabetes without complications); coders locate a term in the **Alphabetic Index** and confirm it in the **Tabular List**, following guidelines and coding to the highest specificity. **Procedure codes** answer “what” was done. **CPT** (Current Procedural Terminology), maintained by the **AMA**, uses **five-digit numeric** Category I codes organized into sections: **Evaluation and Management (E/M)**, Anesthesia, Surgery, Radiology, Pathology and Laboratory and Medicine; **modifiers** (two characters) explain special circumstances. **HCPCS Level II** codes—a letter followed by four digits—cover supplies, ambulance services, durable medical equipment and some drugs. The diagnosis must support **medical necessity** for each procedure.',
sort('ICD-10-CM, CPT or HCPCS Level II?',['ICD-10-CM (diagnosis)','CPT (procedure)','HCPCS Level II (supplies and services)'],[['E11.9',0],['J45.909',0],['99213',1],['81002',1],['E0114 (crutches)',2],['A0428 (ambulance service)',2]])]],
['Compliance and fraud prevention',[
'Accurate coding is a legal requirement. **Fraud** is intentional deception for payment, such as billing for services not provided or **upcoding** (coding a higher-level service than was performed). **Abuse** is billing that is inconsistent with accepted practice, even without intent. **Unbundling** is billing separately for services that should be reported with a single comprehensive code. The **False Claims Act** penalizes fraudulent billing to government programs, and practices follow **compliance programs** with audits and training. Coders never assign a code that is not supported by documentation—“if it wasn’t documented, it wasn’t done.” Patients should receive clear explanations of their bills, and financial conversations are handled privately and respectfully.',
kase('A coding request',{a:{text:'A provider documents a brief visit but asks you to code a higher-level E/M service “because insurance pays more.” What should you do?',choices:[['Code only what the documentation supports and raise the concern with the compliance officer if needed','b','Correct. Upcoding is fraud.',true],['Code the higher level as asked','b','Coding services not supported by documentation is fraudulent.',false]]},b:{text:'A claim is denied because the diagnosis does not support the procedure. What is the first step?',choices:[['Review the documentation and codes for errors, then correct and resubmit if appropriate','c','Correct. Many denials result from coding or data errors.',true],['Bill the patient for the full amount immediately','c','Investigate the denial first.',false]]},c:{text:'You find the diagnosis code was mistyped; you correct it and resubmit a clean claim.',end:true}})]],
["Practice finances, collections and bookkeeping",["Medical assistants often handle the practice’s daily finances. Each patient’s charges, payments and adjustments are posted to their **account** and to a daily summary (**day sheet** or daily report). **Accounts receivable** are amounts owed to the practice; an **aging report** groups unpaid balances by how long they have been outstanding—0–30, 31–60, 61–90 and more than 90 days—so staff can follow up. Payments are recorded accurately with receipts, cash drawers and **petty cash** are reconciled, and deposits are made promptly.","**Collections** follow the law and courtesy. Practices explain financial policies up front, collect co-pays at the time of service and offer **payment plans** when appropriate. Under the **Fair Debt Collection Practices Act**, collectors may not harass or threaten, and calls are generally restricted to between **8 a.m. and 9 p.m.** at the debtor’s location; staff speak only with the responsible party and protect privacy. Accounts may be sent to a collection agency after repeated attempts, according to policy. Adjustments and write-offs are documented and approved.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Appropriate collection practice?","buckets":["Appropriate","Inappropriate"],"items":[["Explaining financial policies at registration",0],["Offering a payment plan",0],["Calling a patient about a balance at 10:30 p.m.",1],["Discussing a balance with the patient’s neighbor",1],["Following up on 61–90-day balances",0],["Threatening a patient with arrest",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Deductible: amount the patient pays each year before insurance pays; co-pay is fixed per visit; coinsurance is a percentage; premiums keep coverage.','Medicare (A hospital, B outpatient, C Advantage, D drugs), Medicaid/CHIP, TRICARE, workers’ comp; HMOs require referrals, PPOs allow out-of-network care.','ICD-10-CM diagnoses (3–7 alphanumeric characters), CPT procedures (5 digits, E/M), HCPCS Level II supplies; avoid upcoding and unbundling.'],terms:[['Deductible','Annual amount paid before the insurer pays.'],['Clean claim','Error-free claim that can be paid promptly.'],['Upcoding','Billing a higher-level service than performed.']],mcqs:[q('If a policy has a $75 deductible, the patient:','Pays this amount each year before the insurance company pays','Does not pay the first $75','Deducts it from the bill','Reimburses the physician $75 only','A Blesi sample question.'),q('A fixed amount paid at each visit is a:','Copayment','Deductible','Premium','Coinsurance','For example, $25 per visit.'),q('Medicare Part D covers:','Prescription drugs','Hospital care','Physician visits','Dental care only','Part A is hospital, Part B outpatient.'),q('Which plan usually requires referrals from a primary care provider?','HMO','PPO','Medicare Part A','Workers’ compensation','The PCP is a gatekeeper.'),q('Professional services are billed on the:','CMS-1500','UB-04','W-2','1099','Hospitals use the UB-04.'),q('Which is an ICD-10-CM code?','E11.9','99213','A0428','J3490 only','Diagnosis codes begin with a letter.'),q('CPT codes are maintained by the:','American Medical Association','CDC','WHO','HOSA','Five-digit procedure codes.'),q('Billing a higher-level service than performed is:','Upcoding','Unbundling','Coordination of benefits','Preauthorization','It is fraud.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient is confused about a bill after a visit. Explain deductible, copayment and coinsurance with an example, and describe how the practice’s billing and coding process produced the bill.',rubric:[['Insurance terms',['deductible','copay','coinsurance','premium','each year']],['Worked example',['$','percent','%','owes','pays']],['Billing cycle',['eligibility','claim','cms-1500','remittance','eob','post']],['Coding',['icd-10','cpt','diagnosis','procedure','medical necessity']]],model:'A deductible is the amount the patient pays each year for covered services before the insurance company begins paying; a copayment is a fixed amount paid at each visit; and coinsurance is a percentage split after the deductible, such as 80/20. For example, if the patient has a $75 copay for the visit, a $500 deductible that is already met and 80/20 coinsurance on a $300 allowed lab charge, she pays the $75 copay at check-in and 20% of $300, or $60, for the lab, while the plan pays $240. The practice verified eligibility before the visit, documented the encounter, coded the diagnosis with ICD-10-CM to show medical necessity and the services with CPT codes such as an E/M visit and the laboratory test, and submitted a CMS-1500 claim. When the insurer’s remittance advice and the patient’s explanation of benefits arrived, the practice posted the payment and billed the patient for her remaining share. I would review the EOB with her privately and answer questions.'},sources:[RULES,HC_GLOSSARY,MEDICARE_PARTS,CMS_ICD,AMA_CPT]};
